## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical edge device security, supply chain controls, and IR readiness for OTA updates in a fleet setting.

## Key Concepts
- Hardware root of trust and device attestation
- Per-release key rotation and role separation
- SBOMs and artifact signing for OTA safety
- OTA gateway revocation and device rollback
- Incident response, containment, and forensics timelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation: verify OTA signature before flash
function verifyAndApply(image, publicKey) {
  const ok = verifySignature(image, publicKey);
  if (!ok) throw new Error('Invalid OTA signature');
  flash(image);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale key rotation across millions of devices?
- What logging and forensics data would you collect for IR?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical containment and preventative controls for cross-account lateral movement in a real-world cloud environment.

## Key Concepts
- AWS account isolation, trust boundary hardening
- MFA, STS short TTLs, per-tenant KMS keys
- IAM Access Analyzer, SCPs, monitoring, and OPA-driven CI checks
- IR/DR framing with defined SLAs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple CI gate: reject policies granting "*" actions or resources
function isLeastPrivileged(policy) {
  const st = policy.Statement || [];
  for (const s of st) {
    const actions = Array.isArray(s.Action) ? s.Action : [s.Action];
    const resources = Array.isArray(s.Resource) ? s.Resource : [s.Resource];
    if (s.Effect === 'Allow' && (actions.includes('*') || resources.includes('*'))) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-account role assumptions in a CI/CD and monitoring loop?
- What metrics indicate lateral movement attempts, and how would you alert on them?
- How would you enforce per-tenant isolation during rapid incident response and remediation?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates practical controls for vendor-integrated cloud data access and cross-service trust boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- PKCE and short-lived tokens prevent token replay and limit exposure windows
- Token binding and mTLS establish bidirectional trust between services
- Dynamic credential rotation minimizes the blast radius of leaked secrets
- Policy-as-code IAM checks enable automated, repeatable security enforcement
- Incident response playbooks ensure rapid containment and recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
const verifyPKCE = (codeChallenge, codeVerifier, method = 'S256') => {
  if (method === 'S256') {
    const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256')
      .update(codeVerifier)
      .digest('base64url');
    return hash === codeChallenge;
  }
  return codeVerifier === codeChallenge;
};

const validateTokenBinding = (token, clientCert, deviceFingerprint) => {
  return token.binding.certThumbprint === clientCert.thumbprint
    && token.binding.deviceId === deviceFingerprint.id;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Examines practical isolation controls for logs in multi-tenant cloud-native systems, including data-minimization, policy enforcement, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped logging
- ABAC/OPA policy enforcement at ingestion
- Data redaction and least-privilege exporters
- SPIRE/mTLS between log agents and collectors
- Ephemeral credentials rotation and secrets hygiene

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactForTenant(log, tenantId) {
  const redacted = {...log};
  const sensitiveKeys = ['ssn','creditCard','dob','email'];
  for (const k of sensitiveKeys) {
    if (redacted[k] && redacted.tenantId === tenantId) {
      redacted[k] = '[REDACTED]';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for cross-tenant access in search results?
- What monitoring signals indicate redaction failures or policy drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable supply chain and third-party risk controls for real-time data platforms, including attestation, signing, SBOM management, and incident response coordination with external vendors.

## Key Concepts
- Supply chain security and third-party risk management
- Artifact signing with Sigstore/cosign and SBOM generation
- Per-artifact IAM and ephemeral credentials via AWS STS
- Runtime attestation for serverless and container environments
- Incident response playbooks, containment strategies, and vendor communications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify artifact signature and SBOM integrity before deployment
async function verifyArtifact(artifactUrl, sbomUrl, expectedDigest) {
  const artifact = await fetch(artifactUrl);
  const artifactDigest = await computeSHA256(artifact);

  if (artifactDigest !== expectedDigest) {
    throw new Error('Artifact integrity check failed');
  }

  const signature = await cosign.verify(artifact, publicKey);
  const sbom = await spdx.parse(fetch(sbomUrl));
  const components = sbom.getVulnerableDependencies();

  return { signature, components, isTrusted: signature.valid };
}
```
